{
  "_accepted_equivalent": "src/backend_scene/src/WPShaderParser.cpp:1150 (cxx_gt_to_ge, cxx_gt_to_le) is `if (s_memoCompiles > 0)`, and it guards nothing but a LOG_INFO summary. Both mutants only change whether that line is emitted, so no assertion can distinguish them without capturing log output, and contorting a test to do so would pin the logging rather than the behaviour. Accepted deliberately. The two sibling survivors that WERE killed (the include-depth bound at :117 and the memo compile counter) are the ones that described real behaviour.  src/backend_scene/src/WPParticleParser.cpp:955 (cxx_gt_to_ge) is the angularmovement drag gate `if (drag > 0.0f)`. `>=` only changes which branch is taken when drag is exactly zero, and there the guarded body computes factor = max(0.0, 1.0 - 0*blend*dt) = 1.0, so MutiplyAngularVelocity(p, 1.0) is the identity on every float value. The only way that factor could come out as anything but 1.0 at drag==0 is a non-finite blend factor or step, and in exactly those cases the operator's own AngularAccelerate(p, force * blend, dt) two lines earlier has already driven angularVelocity to NaN, which absorbs both *1.0 and *0.0 -- so the branch cannot be observed from the outside either way. A non-finite step is not reachable regardless: ParticleSystem clamps frameTime with kMaxParticleFrameTime. Verified empirically as well -- with the mutation applied by hand the whole 3235-case backend_scene_tests suite passes unchanged. The two sibling cxx_mul_to_div mutants on the next line describe real behaviour (blended angular drag) and were killed with tests rather than accepted here.  ||  src/PlaylistManager.cpp:505 (cxx_gt_to_ge) is `const bool forward = delta > 0;`, and the line above it returns on `delta == 0` (`if (m_activeId.isEmpty() || delta == 0) return;`). delta is a plain int, unmodified between the two lines, so at :505 the precondition delta != 0 holds and `> 0` and `>= 0` agree for every reachable value -- they differ only at 0. Every downstream branch is driven solely by `forward`, so nothing can observe the difference. Accepted deliberately.  ||  src/backend_scene/src/VulkanRender/SkyboxPass.cpp:326 (cxx_ne_to_eq) and :334 (cxx_eq_to_ne) are inside SkyboxPass::execute(), which backend_scene_tests never calls -- proven by inserting std::abort() as the function's first statement and watching the whole suite still pass 3257/3257 with exit 0. They survive because the code does not run, not because the behaviour is unpinned, and reaching them needs a live VkDevice plus a compiled render pass, pipeline, mapped dyn_buf, recording command buffer and the push-descriptor extension. Deliberately NOT lifted into a device-free helper: `res != VK_SUCCESS` is the Vulkan API contract, not project policy, and it appears at 42 sites in src/. Extracting this one would create a function whose body is the mutated expression, so the test would restate the wrapper's definition; the mutant would die only because the line moved into a covered translation unit while the branch still never executes.  ||  src/backend_scene/src/Vulkan/include/Vulkan/SwapchainPolicy.hpp:55 (cxx_gt_to_ge) is the second comparison in `if (caps.maxImageCount > 0 && wanted > caps.maxImageCount) return caps.maxImageCount;`. `>=` only changes which branch is taken when wanted == caps.maxImageCount, and there the guarded return hands back caps.maxImageCount -- the same number the fall-through `return wanted` would have produced. The one input that separates the two operators produces an identical result, and every other input takes the same path in both, so nothing downstream can observe the difference. Accepted deliberately. The other `>` on that line is NOT equivalent and stays killed by \"a zero maximum means unlimited\": maxImageCount == 0 means no limit, so `>= 0` would clamp the image count to zero. The four inclusive extent bounds at :42-:44 were killed with boundary cases (a current extent sitting exactly on minImageExtent or maxImageExtent is a size the driver accepts) rather than accepted here.  ||  src/backend_scene/src/Vulkan/include/Vulkan/TextureCacheDetail.hpp:175 (cxx_lt_to_le) is the verbose-head test `if (prior_failures < verbose_head) return true;` in shouldLogUploadFailure, where verbose_head is a constexpr 3. `<=` differs only at prior_failures == 3, and there the unmutated fall-through evaluates (3 - 3) % 600 == 0, which is true -- the same answer the mutant returns directly. prior_failures is the only argument and both constants are constexpr locals, so there is no reachable input that separates the two. Accepted deliberately. The subtraction on the next line does describe real behaviour -- the interval is anchored at the end of the verbose head, so the periodic line lands on 603, not 600 -- and was killed with a test.  ||  src/FileHelper.cpp:155 (cxx_gt_to_ge, cxx_gt_to_le) is the preallocation hint `if (declared > 0) out.reserve(declared);` in readRegularFileCapped. reserve() sets capacity and nothing else. `>=` differs only when declared == 0, and there it calls out.reserve(0) on an empty QByteArray -- a no-op. `<=` differs whenever declared > 0, and there it merely drops the preallocation and lets append() grow the buffer geometrically instead; the bytes appended, the final size and the value returned are the same either way. declared is QFile::size() on an already-opened regular file, so it is never negative and there is no third case. The only thing either mutant can change is the capacity of a buffer that is about to be filled to exactly the same size -- how many reallocations happen, not what the function returns -- and pinning it would mean asserting on QByteArray::capacity(), which is not part of what the read promises. Accepted deliberately. The sibling guards on the same read path do describe real behaviour and were killed with tests rather than accepted here: the up-front cap at :144 by a file measuring exactly kMaxReadSize, the mid-read cap at :164 by a file that gains a byte after the size check, and the loop's error and EOF tests at :159 and :163 by the existing round-trip reads.",
  "_comment": "Surviving mutants accepted as baseline. Run tools/scripts/mutation.sh --refresh-baseline to update; new entries in a non-refresh run fail the gate.",
  "_scope": "Measured from the parent test binaries only. backend_scene_tests is NOT represented: its instrumented warmup takes ~91s and Mull's ceiling was 60s from 2026-06-06 until 2026-09-01, so that target has never once produced a report. The src/backend_scene/ entries here come from parent binaries linking submodule sources (Audio, Utils), not from that target. A future full submodule sweep will therefore surface a large batch of survivors that are first measurements, not regressions -- refresh the baseline from that run rather than treating them as new.  Since 2026-09-04 the parent's own tests/ directory is excluded in tests/mull.yml: Mull had been mutating the test sources themselves and 163 of the entries here sat in tests/*.cpp, which say nothing about the product. Those entries were pruned when the exclude landed.",
